{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031","statement":"An accessory navicular bone is a small bone located in the middle of the foot, near the navicular bone.","source_span":"An accessory navicular bone is a small bone located in the middle of the foot. It is near the navicular bone"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031","statement":"Accessory navicular bone is estimated to occur in approximately 2 to 12% of the general population.","source_span":"estimated to be in approximately 2 to 12% of the general population"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031","statement":"Accessory navicular bone occurs in up to 14% of children.","source_span":"up to 14% of children"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031","statement":"This bone may develop a bump that can cause irritation, swelling, and pain.","source_span":"This bone may develop a bump that can cause irritation, swelling, and pain."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031","statement":"Symptoms of accessory navicular bone may worsen with increased activity or tight shoes.","source_span":"Symptoms may worsen with increased activity or tight shoes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031","statement":"The cause of accessory navicular bone is unknown, but may be related to flatfoot (pes planus) or repeated foot and ankle sprains.","source_span":"the condition may be related to the development of flatfoot also known as pes planus, in other cases it may be related to repeated foot and ankle sprains"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031","statement":"In children, accessory navicular bone symptoms usually resolve once the child stops growing.","source_span":"In children the condition usually resolves once the child stops growing."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031","statement":"For people with severe symptoms, surgery may be considered to remove the bony growth.","source_span":"For people with accessory navicular bone who experience severe symptoms surgery may be considered to remove the bony growth."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000031","statement":"Treatments for accessory navicular bone may include NSAIDs such as ibuprofen, moleskin padding, or immobilizing the area with a cast for six weeks.","source_span":"non-steroidal anti-inflammatories (NSAIDs) such as ibuprofen, placing a doughnut-shaped piece of moleskin around the affected area to relieve pain and tenderness, or immobilizing the area with a cast for six weeks"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is an accessory navicular bone and where is it located in the foot?","answer":"It is a small bone located in the middle of the foot, near the navicular bone (the bone that crosses the foot near the instep)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"How common is an accessory navicular bone in the general population versus in children?","answer":"It occurs in an estimated 2 to 12% of the general population and up to 14% of children."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What is thought to cause accessory navicular bone?","answer":"The exact cause is unknown, but it may be related to flatfoot (pes planus) or repeated foot and ankle sprains."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031","fact_refs":[9],"question":"How long is a cast typically used to immobilize an accessory navicular bone that is causing symptoms?","answer":"The area may be immobilized with a cast for six weeks."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What non-surgical treatments are used for a painful accessory navicular bone?","answer":"Options include restricting activities, wearing softer or specially made shoes, NSAIDs like ibuprofen, moleskin padding around the area, or immobilizing with a cast for six weeks."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000031","fact_refs":[6,7],"question":"Does accessory navicular bone in children require surgery?","answer":"Not usually — in children, the condition typically resolves on its own once the child stops growing, though softer shoes or shoe supports may be used for symptoms."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034","statement":"Aceruloplasminemia is a disorder of iron metabolism that causes iron to build up in the body.","source_span":"Aceruloplasminemia is a disorder of iron metabolism. This disorder causes iron to build-up in the body."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034","statement":"People with aceruloplasminemia tend to develop anemia and diabetes in their 20's.","source_span":"People with this disorder tend to develop anemia and diabetes in their 20's."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034","statement":"As aceruloplasminemia progresses, movement problems such as tremors, chorea, ataxia, eyelid twitching, and grimacing are common.","source_span":"movement problems are common, such as tremors, chorea, ataxia, eyelid twitching, and grimacing"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034","statement":"Some people with aceruloplasminemia experience psychiatric problems and dementia in their 40's and 50's.","source_span":"Some experience psychiatric problems and dementia in their 40's and 50's."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034","statement":"Aceruloplasminemia is caused by mutations in the CP gene and is inherited in an autosomal recessive fashion.","source_span":"Aceruloplasminemia is caused by mutations in the CP gene and are inherited in an autosomal recessive fashion."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034","statement":"Abnormal renal physiology, abnormality of iron homeostasis, anemia, chorea, diabetes mellitus, retinopathy, and tremor each occur in about 90% of people with aceruloplasminemia.","source_span":"Abnormal renal physiology 90% Abnormality of iron homeostasis 90% Anemia 90% Chorea 90% Diabetes mellitus 90% Retinopathy 90% Tremor 90%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034","statement":"People with aceruloplasminemia tend to have low serum copper (<10 ug/dL), low serum iron (<45 ug/dL), high serum ferritin (850-4000 ng/mL), and absent serum ceruloplasmin concentration.","source_span":"People with aceruloplasminemia tend to have low serum copper (<10 ug/dL), low serum iron (< 45 ug/dL), high serum ferritin (850-4000 ng/mL) and absent serum ceruloplasmin concentration."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034","statement":"Aceruloplasminemia should be suspected when a person has more than one of: diabetes mellitus, retinal degeneration, anemia, and movement disorder.","source_span":"When a person has more than one of the following symptoms, aceruloplasminemia should be suspected: Diabetes mellitus Retinal degeneration Anemia Movement disorder"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000034","statement":"Diagnosis of aceruloplasminemia can be further supported by MRI and pathology results demonstrating iron deposition in the body.","source_span":"Diagnosis can be further supported by MRI and pathology results demonstrating iron deposition in the body."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000034","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What gene is mutated in aceruloplasminemia and how is it inherited?","answer":"It is caused by mutations in the CP gene and is inherited in an autosomal recessive fashion."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000034","fact_refs":[1],"question":"At what age do people with aceruloplasminemia typically develop anemia and diabetes?","answer":"They tend to develop anemia and diabetes in their 20s."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000034","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What lab findings are typical in a person with aceruloplasminemia?","answer":"Low serum copper (<10 ug/dL), low serum iron (<45 ug/dL), high serum ferritin (850-4000 ng/mL), and absent serum ceruloplasmin concentration."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000034","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Which symptoms occur in about 90% of people with aceruloplasminemia?","answer":"Abnormal renal physiology, abnormality of iron homeostasis, anemia, chorea, diabetes mellitus, retinopathy, and tremor each occur in about 90% of patients."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000034","fact_refs":[7],"question":"When should a doctor suspect aceruloplasminemia in a patient?","answer":"It should be suspected when a person has more than one of: diabetes mellitus, retinal degeneration, anemia, or a movement disorder."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000034","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What psychiatric or cognitive symptoms can develop in aceruloplasminemia, and at what age?","answer":"Some people develop psychiatric problems and dementia, typically in their 40s and 50s."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037","statement":"Achalasia is typically diagnosed in individuals between 25 and 60 years of age.","source_span":"Achalasia is typically diagnosed in individuals between 25 and 60 years of age."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037","statement":"Achalasia symptoms are caused by damage to the nerves of the esophagus, though the exact etiology is unknown.","source_span":"The exact etiology is unknown, however, symptoms are caused by damage to the nerves of the esophagus."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037","statement":"When a genetic influence is suspected, achalasia is called familial esophageal achalasia.","source_span":"When a genetic influence is suspected, achalasia is called familial esophageal achalasia."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037","statement":"Most people with achalasia experience difficulty swallowing (dysphagia) and heartburn.","source_span":"Most people with achalasia experience difficulty swallowing, also known as dysphagia and heartburn."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037","statement":"Other achalasia symptoms include regurgitation/vomiting, noncardiac chest pain, odynophagia, upper abdominal pain, coughing/asthma, chronic aspiration, hoarseness/sore throat, and unintentional weight loss.","source_span":"regurgitation or vomiting, noncardiac chest pain, odynophagia (painful swallowing), and pain in the upper central region of the abdomen. Non esophageal symptoms might include: coughing or asthma, chronic aspiration ... hoarseness or sore throat, and unintentional weight loss"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037","statement":"The diagnosis of achalasia is confirmed by manometry, though upper endoscopy and upper GI X-ray can also be useful.","source_span":"The diagnosis of achalasia is confirmed by manometry (test that measures how well the esophagus is working); however, other tests such as upper endoscopy and upper GI X-ray can additionally be useful."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000037","statement":"Achalasia treatment includes Botox injection (temporary), medications like isosorbide dinitrate or nifedipine, Heller myotomy surgery, and pneumatic balloon dilation.","source_span":"Injection with botulinum toxin (Botox) ... Medications, such as long-acting nitrates (i.e. isosorbide dinitrate) or calcium channel blockers (i.e. nifedipine) ... Surgery (Heller myotomy) ... Pneumatic balloon dilation of the esophagus"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037","fact_refs":[0],"question":"At what age range is achalasia typically diagnosed?","answer":"Achalasia is typically diagnosed in individuals between 25 and 60 years of age."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What test is used to confirm a diagnosis of achalasia?","answer":"The diagnosis is confirmed by manometry, though upper endoscopy and upper GI X-ray can also be useful."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What are the two most common symptoms of achalasia?","answer":"Most people with achalasia experience difficulty swallowing (dysphagia) and heartburn."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What surgical option is available for achalasia and what is it called?","answer":"Surgery called a Heller myotomy can be performed to decrease the pressure in the lower esophageal sphincter."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What medications are used to relax the lower esophageal sphincter in achalasia treatment?","answer":"Long-acting nitrates such as isosorbide dinitrate, or calcium channel blockers such as nifedipine."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000037","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What is familial esophageal achalasia?","answer":"It is the term used for achalasia when a genetic influence is suspected."}
